  "title": "Epigenetics and Plasticity",
  "emoji": "🔄",
  "color": "#7E22CE",
  "description": "A short lesson to help you apply Epigenetics and Plasticity.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Two identical twins grow up in different environments and decades later their brains look nothing alike despite identical DNA.",
      "visual": "👯"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Epigenetics explains how experience changes gene expression without altering the DNA sequence itself, by adding or removing.",
      "visual": "🏷️"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "When you learn something new, epigenetic marks such as methyl groups and histone modifications change which genes get expressed.",
      "visual": "🧬"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Some epigenetic changes are long-lasting.",
      "visual": "🔇"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Researchers have reversed maladaptive epigenetic marks in animal models using targeted drugs, restoring normal plasticity.",
      "visual": "💉"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Your DNA is the blueprint, but experience holds the pen that decides which lines get read.",
      "visual": "🖊️"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "How do epigenetic changes affect neuroplasticity?",
    "options": [
      "They alter which plasticity genes are active without changing DNA",
      "They permanently delete genes from neurons",
      "They have no relationship to brain plasticity"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
